>
  <main class="qc-page">
    <!-- =====================================================
         HERO
    ====================================================== -->
    <header class="qc-hero">
      <div class="qc-shell">
        <div class="qc-hero__grid">
          <div class="qc-hero__copy">
            <p class="qc-hero__overline">
              Deep dive 06 · Quality &amp; reliability
            </p>

            <h1 class="qc-hero__title">
              Quality Control
              <span>&amp; Tolerances</span>
            </h1>

            <p class="qc-hero__desc">{meta.excerpt}</p>

            <div class="qc-hero__meta">
              <time datetime={toNewsISO(meta.date)}>
                {formatNewsDate(meta.date)}
              </time>
              <span class="qc-hero__dot"></span>
              <span>METALCRAFTS</span>
              <span class="qc-hero__dot"></span>
              <span>Deep dive · 06 of 06</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="qc-hero__visual">
            <div class="qc-hero__frame">
              <Image
                src={qcShot}
                alt="Inspection reference"
                loading="eager"
                widths={[720, 1080, 1440]}
                sizes="(max-width: 820px) 100vw, 52vw"
              />
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              <span>Inspection &amp; measurement</span>
              <span>Reference</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- =====================================================
         ARTICLE
    ====================================================== -->
    <section class="qc-article">
      <div class="qc-shell">
        <div class="qc-reading">
          <!-- Desktop contents -->
          <aside class="qc-sidebar" aria-label="Article contents">
            <div class="qc-sidebar__sticky">
              <p class="qc-sidebar__heading">In this deep dive</p>

              <nav class="qc-toc">
                {sections.map((item) => (
                  <a
                    href={`#${item.id}`}
                    class="qc-toc__link"
                    data-toc-link={item.id}
                  >
                    <span class="qc-toc__number">{item.number}</span>
                    <span class="qc-toc__text">{item.title}</span>
                  </a>
                ))}
              </nav>

              <div class="qc-sidebar__note">
                <span class="qc-sidebar__mark">MC</span>
                <div class="qc-sidebar__note-body">
                  <p>
                    The last of six. If the earlier guides describe how a part
                    is made, this one describes how it is accepted.
                  </p>

                  <a
                    href="/resources/metal-craft-guide"
                    class="qc-sidebar__back"
                  >
                    <span aria-hidden="true">←</span>
                    Back to the full guide
                  </a>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </aside>

          <!-- Body -->
          <article class="qc-content">
            <!-- Mobile contents -->
            <details class="qc-mobile-toc">
              <summary>
                <span>In this deep dive</span>
                <span class="qc-mobile-toc__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
              </summary>
              <nav aria-label="Mobile article contents">
                {sections.map((item) => (
                  <a href={`#${item.id}`}>
                    <span>{item.number}</span>
                    {item.title}
                  </a>
                ))}
              </nav>
            </details>

            <!-- ---------- THESIS ---------- -->
            <section class="qc-thesis" aria-label="The short answer">
              <span class="qc-thesis__label">The short answer</span>
              <p>
                A batch is not good because someone looked at it. It is good
                because the defect classes, the sample size and the decision rule
                were agreed before the first piece was made — and because the
                next batch can be compared with this one on the same numbers.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 01 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="system"
              class="qc-section"
              data-article-section="system"
            >
              <header class="qc-section__header">
                <span class="qc-section__index">01</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="qc-section__eyebrow">Principle</p>
                  <h2>Checking is not a system</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                “We check every piece” is a statement about effort. It is not a
                statement about method, and the two are not the same thing. A
                hundred percent inspection has its own failure modes: an
                inspector working through a tray of five thousand pieces stops
                seeing the fifth defect, applies the standard differently on
                Thursday than on Monday, and leaves behind no record that anyone
                can argue with later.
              </p>

              <p>
                Sampling has different failure modes, and they are at least
                visible. It accepts a known risk, in exchange for a rule that two
                people can apply the same way: a defined sample size, defects
                sorted into classes, an acceptance number, and a record.
              </p>

              <p>
                So the useful question is not “how much do you inspect”. It is:
                what would let a buyer accept a lot they have never seen? The
                answer has to be a system, because a promise cannot be checked
                and a system can.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-note">
                <p class="qc-note__label">What this page is for</p>
                <p class="qc-note__foot">
                  The five earlier guides describe how a part is made and what
                  can go wrong at each stage. This one describes how a batch is
                  accepted: what is measured, how many pieces, by which rule, and
                  what happens when the rule says no.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 02 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="tolerance"
              class="qc-section"
              data-article-section="tolerance"
            >
              <header class="qc-section__header">
                <span class="qc-section__index">02</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="qc-section__eyebrow">Tolerance</p>
                  <h2>What a tolerance can promise</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                A tolerance is not a wish. It is the space a process needs in
                order to be repeatable, and that space is created by the process
                itself — long before anyone writes a number on a drawing.
              </p>

              <figure class="qc-figure">
                <div class="qc-figure__frame">
                  <svg
                    viewBox="0 0 660 214"
                    role="img"
                    aria-labelledby="tol-title tol-desc"
                  >
                    <title id="tol-title">
                      A tolerance band with a process distribution inside it
                    </title>
                    <desc id="tol-desc">
                      The band runs between an upper and a lower limit. A stable
                      process sits inside it with room on both sides.
                    </desc>

                    <rect
                      x="80"
                      y="70"
                      width="480"
                      height="80"
                      fill="var(--color-gold)"
                      opacity="0.08"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                      stroke-dasharray="5 4"
                    ></rect>

                    <line
                      x1="80"
                      y1="110"
                      x2="560"
                      y2="110"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                      stroke-dasharray="2 5"
                    ></line>

                    <path
                      d="M230 150 C 275 150, 290 84, 320 84 C 350 84, 365 150, 410 150"
                      fill="none"
                      stroke="var(--color-gold)"
                      stroke-width="1.5"
                    ></path>

                    <text
                      x="80"
                      y="60"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      upper limit
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="80"
                      y="168"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      lower limit
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="80"
                      y="104"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      nominal
                    </text>

                    <text
                      x="320"
                      y="196"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      a stable process leaves room on both sides
                    </text>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <figcaption>
                  The band is the specification. The curve is what the process
                  actually does. A tolerance that the curve cannot fit inside is
                  not a quality improvement.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <p>
                Where the space comes from: a die wears, so dimensions drift as
                the tool runs; plating adds to every surface it touches, so a
                plated dimension is not the machined dimension; casting needs
                draft and shrinks as it cools; hard enamel is ground back after
                filling, which removes material from the face. Each of those is a
                physical allowance, and none of them can be argued away by
                tightening a number.
              </p>

              <p>
                This is why the same drawing gets a different tolerance from a
                stamped part and a cast part. It is also why a drawing should
                say which dimensions are functional — a hole position, a stem
                diameter, an overall size — and which are cosmetic. When every
                dimension carries the same tight tolerance, the inspection bench
                ends up enforcing a standard the process was never able to meet,
                and the argument moves from the drawing to the loading dock.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 03 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="weights"
              class="qc-section"
              data-article-section="weights"
            >
              <header class="qc-section__header">
                <span class="qc-section__index">03</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="qc-section__eyebrow">Classification</p>
                  <h2>Defects have three weights</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Before any sampling plan means anything, the defects have to be
                sorted. A scratch on the back and a clutch that lets go are not
                the same event, and counting them in one column makes the
                acceptance number meaningless.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-weights">
                {weights.map((item) => (
                  <div class="qc-weight">
                    <span class="qc-weight__num">{item.num}</span>
                    <h3>{item.label}</h3>
                    <p>{item.text}</p>
                    <p class="qc-weight__rule">{item.rule}</p>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                The classification has to be agreed before the inspection, not
                after a dispute. It is the only way two inspectors, in two
                places, reach the same count — and the only way a rejection can
                be explained without appealing to taste. If a supplier and a
                buyer cannot agree on which class a defect belongs to, they are
                not going to agree on the verdict either.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 04 ---------- -->
            <section id="aql" class="qc-section" data-article-section="aql">
              <header class="qc-section__header">
                <span class="qc-section__index">04</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="qc-section__eyebrow">Sampling</p>
                  <h2>AQL: from sample to decision</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                AQL is the most quoted and least understood number in this
                trade. It is not “the percentage of defects we allow”. It is the
                quality level at which a lot still has a defined, low probability
                of being rejected — a rule for turning a sample into a decision,
                with the risk stated rather than hidden.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-chain">
                {chain.map((item) => (
                  <div class="qc-chain__step">
                    <span class="qc-chain__num">{item.num}</span>
                    <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                    <p>{item.text}</p>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                Two risks sit inside that rule. A good lot can still be rejected
                — that is the producer's risk — and a bad lot can still be
                accepted, which is the buyer's. A tighter AQL reduces the second
                and increases the first, and it also increases the sample size.
                There is no setting where both risks disappear; there is only a
                setting where both are known.
              </p>

              <p>
                Which is why the level has to be named in the order. The
                standard, the inspection level and the AQL for each defect class
                are agreed per order — usually the buyer's specification, or the
                one written into the quotation. When nobody names them, the
                inspection has no decision rule, and what is left is an opinion
                with a clipboard.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-note">
                <p class="qc-note__label">What AQL cannot tell you</p>
                <p class="qc-note__foot">
                  It says nothing about the next lot, and nothing about whether
                  the process is stable. It answers one question only: given this
                  sample, should this lot be accepted? The next section is about
                  the question it leaves open.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 05 ---------- -->
            <section id="points" class="qc-section" data-article-section="points">
              <header class="qc-section__header">
                <span class="qc-section__index">05</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="qc-section__eyebrow">Inspection</p>
                  <h2>The four inspection points</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Inspection is not one event at the end. It is four events, and
                each one catches something the others cannot.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-points">
                {points.map((item) => (
                  <div class="qc-point">
                    <span class="qc-point__num">{item.num}</span>
                    <div>
                      <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                      <p>{item.text}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                The economics of the four points are not equal. A fault caught at
                incoming material costs a phone call; the same fault caught at
                pre-shipment costs a sorted lot, a delayed container, and an
                argument about who pays for the sort. That is the entire reason
                to inspect early, and it has nothing to do with distrust.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 06 ---------- -->
            <section id="report" class="qc-section" data-article-section="report">
              <header class="qc-section__header">
                <span class="qc-section__index">06</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="qc-section__eyebrow">Evidence</p>
                  <h2>What a useful report contains</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                An inspection that leaves no record is a conversation. A record
                that cannot be tied to a carton is a story. What a buyer needs is
                a document that answers “which pieces, measured how, against
                what, and what did you find”.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-report">
                <div class="qc-report__head">
                  <span class="qc-report__title">Inspection record</span>
                  <span class="qc-report__stamp">Batch linked</span>
                </div>

                <div class="qc-report__rows">
                  {reportFields.map((item) => (
                    <div class="qc-report__row">
                      <span class="qc-report__label">{item.label}</span>
                      <p>{item.text}</p>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </div>

              <p>
                The two fields people leave out are the two that matter most in
                an argument: the sample size, and photographs of the defects
                that were found. A report that only shows the good pieces proves
                nothing except that the photographer chose well.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 07 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="consistency"
              class="qc-section"
              data-article-section="consistency"
            >
              <header class="qc-section__header">
                <span class="qc-section__index">07</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="qc-section__eyebrow">Drift</p>
                  <h2>Consistency is not conformance</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                AQL compares a lot with a standard. Consistency compares lots
                with each other. Those are different questions, and a supplier
                can pass the first one for a very long time while failing the
                second.
              </p>

              <figure class="qc-figure">
                <div class="qc-figure__frame">
                  <svg
                    viewBox="0 0 660 250"
                    role="img"
                    aria-labelledby="drift-title drift-desc"
                  >
                    <title id="drift-title">
                      Six lots, all inside tolerance, drifting upward
                    </title>
                    <desc id="drift-desc">
                      Every lot mean sits inside the band, but the trend moves
                      toward the upper limit with each shipment.
                    </desc>

                    <rect
                      x="70"
                      y="70"
                      width="530"
                      height="110"
                      fill="var(--color-gold)"
                      opacity="0.06"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                      stroke-dasharray="5 4"
                    ></rect>

                    <text
                      x="70"
                      y="60"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      upper limit
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="70"
                      y="196"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      lower limit
                    </text>

                    <polyline
                      points="100,158 180,152 260,144 340,132 420,116 500,100"
                      fill="none"
                      stroke="var(--color-gold)"
                      stroke-width="1.5"
                    ></polyline>

                    <circle cx="100" cy="158" r="3.5" fill="var(--color-gold)"></circle>
                    <circle cx="180" cy="152" r="3.5" fill="var(--color-gold)"></circle>
                    <circle cx="260" cy="144" r="3.5" fill="var(--color-gold)"></circle>
                    <circle cx="340" cy="132" r="3.5" fill="var(--color-gold)"></circle>
                    <circle cx="420" cy="116" r="3.5" fill="var(--color-gold)"></circle>
                    <circle cx="500" cy="100" r="3.5" fill="var(--color-gold)"></circle>

                    <text
                      x="100"
                      y="222"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      lot 1
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="180"
                      y="222"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      lot 2
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="260"
                      y="222"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      lot 3
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="340"
                      y="222"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      lot 4
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="420"
                      y="222"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      lot 5
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="500"
                      y="222"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="9"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      lot 6
                    </text>

                    <text
                      x="600"
                      y="140"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="end"
                    >
                      every lot passes
                    </text>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <figcaption>
                  Six shipments, six accepted lots, one trend. Nothing here is a
                  rejection — and nothing here is stable.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <p>
                This is what drift looks like from inside a quality system. A
                die wears, a bath depletes, a pigment lot changes, an operator
                compensates a little. Every individual lot can be inside the
                band while the process walks toward one edge of it. Then one
                shipment crosses the line, and to everyone on the buyer's side it
                arrives without warning.
              </p>

              <p>
                Seeing it needs a different instrument: pick two or three
                functional characteristics, measure the same one on every lot,
                and plot the readings against the band over time. The trend
                appears long before the failure. That is also the answer to the
                most common complaint in this trade — “the second batch doesn't
                match the first”. It usually matched the standard exactly.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-note">
                <p class="qc-note__label">Why colour and plating drift first</p>
                <p class="qc-note__foot">
                  They are the two characteristics with the most inputs: pigment
                  batch, bath chemistry, plating thickness, polishing time. They
                  are also the two the buyer notices first, which is why a
                  consistency chart is worth more than another inspection.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 08 ---------- -->
            <section id="fail" class="qc-section" data-article-section="fail">
              <header class="qc-section__header">
                <span class="qc-section__index">08</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="qc-section__eyebrow">Response</p>
                  <h2>When a batch fails</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                A rejection is not the end of the system — it is the moment the
                system either works or doesn't. Four steps, in this order, and
                the order matters more than the speed.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-fail">
                {failSteps.map((item) => (
                  <div class="qc-fail__step">
                    <span class="qc-fail__num">{item.num}</span>
                    <div>
                      <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                      <p>{item.text}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                The part people skip is the last one. A failure that is fixed but
                not recorded is a failure that will be repeated, usually on the
                next order, usually with a different person explaining it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- CLOSING ---------- -->
            <footer class="qc-conclusion">
              <div class="qc-conclusion__rule"></div>
              <p class="qc-conclusion__label">The decision</p>

              <div class="qc-rule">
                <p>
                  <strong>Agree the classes before the inspection.</strong>
                  Critical, major, minor — written down, with examples. Without
                  that, two inspectors cannot produce the same number.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Name the standard, the level and the sample size.</strong>
                  An AQL without a level is an adjective; with one, it is a
                  decision rule that both sides can apply.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Judge the series, not only the lot.</strong> Conformance
                  asks whether this batch is acceptable. Consistency asks whether
                  it is the same product you approved.
                </p>
              </div>

              <p class="qc-conclusion__text">
                That is the end of the six. If you have a batch on the water, or
                a first article waiting for a decision, send the numbers — the
                measurements, the sample size, and what you were told. Reading
                the record is usually faster than inspecting again.
              </p>

              <div class="qc-conclusion__signature">
                <span>METALCRAFTS</span>
                <span>CRAFT · DETAIL · QUALITY</span>
              </div>

